  "rationale": "At 10 days of age with bilateral mucopurulent conjunctivitis, chlamydial ophthalmia neonatorum is the most likely diagnosis.",
  "optionExplanations": {
    "A": "Usually causes tearing and discharge without true conjunctival inflammation; not the best fit for mucopurulent conjunctivitis.",
    "B": "Correct: typical timing for chlamydial ophthalmia neonatorum is 5 to 14 days after birth.",
    "C": "Usually presents earlier, often within 2 to 5 days, and can be severe with profuse purulent discharge.",
    "D": "Can cause neonatal eye disease, but this presentation and timing are more typical of chlamydial conjunctivitis.",
    "E": "Possible cause of conjunctivitis, but not the classic neonatal cause suggested by this timing."
  },
